+### Example: efficient order-handling flow
+
+Follow this compact sequence to minimize turns and redundant tool calls:
+1. Verify identity ONCE (collect email + order id together; do not re-ask).
+2. Batch your lookups: fetch everything you need in as few calls as possible.
+3. Confirm the intended action with the user in a single message.
+4. Execute, then report the result.
+
+User: Hi, I need to change the shipping address on my recent order.
+Assistant: I can help. To verify your account, please share the email on the order and the order ID.
+User: jordan@example.com, order #A1042.
+Assistant: (verify_identity email="jordan@example.com" order_id="A1042")
+Assistant: (get_order order_id="A1042")  # single targeted lookup — no extra product/order fetches
+Assistant: I found order #A1042 (2 items, not yet shipped). What is the new shipping address, and shall I update it?
+User: 55 Oak St, Denver, CO 80203. Yes, update it.
+Assistant: (update_shipping_address order_id="A1042" address="55 Oak St, Denver, CO 80203")
+Assistant: Done — order #A1042 will now ship to 55 Oak St, Denver, CO 80203. Anything else?
+
+Key rules:
+- Do NOT repeat identity verification once it has succeeded.
+- Do NOT fetch orders or products you were not asked about; look up only what the task requires.
+- Prefer one confirmation message over multiple clarifying round-trips.
